## 1. Introduction
MOFs consisting of metal clusters and organic linkers have a wide range of potential applications, which include gas adsorption/separation, catalysis, magnetism, proton conduction and drug delivery [1–9]. A crucial issue facing the applications of MOFs is their stability in water [10–13]. For example, use of the MOFs such as HKUST-1 and MOF-5 in aqueous solution is impracticable because they are unstable in water [14,15]. Titanium is often chosen to construct MOFs for its high catalytic activity, but Ti-based MOFs are somewhat unstable in water [16–18]. The structure of a Ti-based MOF usually collapses gradually after being stirred in water, which in turn decreases its photocatalytic activity [19]. In designing stable MOFs, the hard soft acid base concept is often invoked [20]. Here, we report a very simple and efficient method to enhance the stability of a representative Ti-based MOF, Ti-ATA $\mathrm{\ATA}=2$ -aminoterephthallate ion) in water for days, without sacrificing its porosity and photocatalytic activity. The stability of Ti-ATA in water is greatly improved when it is treated with aqueous solutions containing metal ions $\mathbf{M}^{n+}$ with empty d orbitals $(\Upsilon^{3+},\mathrm{La}^{3+},\mathrm{Ce}^{3+},\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ ) or partially-filled d orbitals $(\mathbf{Cr^{3+}})$ .
## 2. Results and discussion
For convenience of our discussion, we use the notation Ti-ATAM to refer to the Ti-ATA treated with aqueous solution containing the metal ions $\mathbb{M}^{n+}$ $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\nabla^{3+}}$ , ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ ). We first compare the structures of Ti-ATA and Ti-ATA-Cr. As shown in Fig. 1a, the powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of Ti-ATA-Cr are identical to that of the parent Ti-ATA [19], indicating that Ti-ATA-Cr and Ti-ATA have the same framework structure, and the Cr of Ti-ATA-Cr does not substitute for the Ti in Ti-ATA. The energy dispersive X-ray spectrum (EDS) of Ti-ATA-Cr proves the presence of Cr in Ti-ATA-Cr (Fig. S1) with the $\mathrm{{Cr/Ti}}$ atom ratio of 0.16. The latter is the maximum amount of Cr that one can upload in Ti-ATA, because this ratio is not further increased even when more ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ ions are added in the starting solution. This suggests that only the surfaces of Ti-ATA particles are affected by ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ ions. The content of ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ in Ti-ATA-Cr was also determined using ICP-AES. The concentration of Ti and Cr were determined to be 29.55 and $5.13\mathrm{mg/L}$ , respectively under our experimental conditions. Therefore, the $\mathrm{Cr/Ti}$ atom ratio was 0.16, which was consistent with the EDS data.
The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of Ti-ATA and Ti-ATA-Cr are presented in Fig. 1b–d. Even after 4 days of being stirred in water, the morphology and particle size of Ti-ATA-Cr still remain the same as those of the as-prepared Ti-ATA. Whereas, the nanocube morphology of the as-prepared Ti-ATA is destroyed into much smaller nanoparticles after 4 days of being stirred in water. The stabilizing effect of Cr on Ti-ATA is further checked under harsh conditions, namely, hydrothermal treatment at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ as well as stirring in acidic $\langle\mathsf{p H}=3.0\rangle$ , neutral $\mathrm{\Phi(pH=7.0)}$ and alkaline $\mathrm{\langlepH}=11$ ) solutions for 10, 15 and 20 days. The powder XRD patterns reveal that Ti-ATA is unstable, but Ti-ATA-Cr remains stable even under these harsh conditions (Fig. S2).
To check how the porous structure of Ti-ATA is modified by the introduction of Cr, we compare the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ of Ti-ATA and Ti-ATA-Cr (Fig. 1e). At high relative pressure $\mathrm{P/P_{0}},$ both Ti-ATA and Ti-ATA-Cr show a high adsorption, indicating the presence of accumulation pores. Both samples display typical type-IV isotherms with a type H3 hysteresis loop, indicating the existence of irregular mesoporous structure. Compared with Ti-ATA, Ti-ATA-Cr displays an obvious promotion of mesoporosity, which is beneficial to improve the diffusion of small guest molecule, and restrain the decomposition of the MOF framework structure [21,22]. Therefore, it is reasonable that Ti-ATA-Cr is more stable than Ti-ATA in water. The BET surface area of Ti-ATA and Ti-ATA-Cr are calculated to be 1053.3 and $920.2\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ , respectively. Thus, the introduction of Cr preserves the porous framework structure of Ti-ATA with only a small decrease in the BET surface area.
Ti-ATA has been applied to photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). The photocatalytic HER activities over Ti-ATA and Ti-ATA-Cr show that Ti-ATA-Cr and Ti-ATA exhibit the same HER rate in the first two hours (Fig. 2). The HER rate over Ti-ATA decreases gradually so that there is almost no increase in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution after $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ of reaction, implying the collapse of the framework structure of Ti-ATA. However, Ti-ATA-Cr displays steady and stable $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution even with $20\mathrm{h}$ of continuous reaction. Consequently, the introduction of Cr does not decrease the photocatalytic activity of Ti-ATA, and Ti-ATA-Cr is indeed much more stable than Ti-ATA.
To search for other ions that can improve the stability of Ti-ATA against water, we investigated the effects of the transition-metal ions such as $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , $C o^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and $\Upsilon^{3+}$ . The powder XRD patterns show that $\Upsilon^{3+}$ has the same effect as does ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ (Fig. 3 and S3). The electronic configurations of the ions $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}(3\mathrm{d}^{3})$ and $\Upsilon^{3+}(4\mathsf{d}^{0})$ suggest that the metal ions with partially-filled or low-lying empty ${\mathsf{d}}{\mathsf{\Omega}}$ -orbitals can stabilize Ti-ATA in water. This suggestion implies that ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{N}\mathsf{d}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ ions with empty 5d orbitals should also stabilize Ti-ATA in water. To check this point, we examined the Ti-ATA-M $(\mathbb{M}^{n+}=\mathbb{L}\mathsf{a}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ ) systems. As expected, both the power XRD patterns and the nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms of Ti-ATA-M suggest that Ti-ATA maintains the framework structure once treated with these four ions $\mathbf{M}^{n+}$ (Fig. S4, S5). From these results, we conclude that transitionmetal and rare-earth ions with empty or partially-filled dorbitals stabilize Ti-ATA in water.
In general, the stability of a material in water increases with increasing its hydrophobicity. The latter can be estimated by measuring the contact angle of water on its surface. As shown in Supporting material Fig. S6, the contact angles of water on the surfaces of Ti-ATA-Cr and Ti-ATA are $36.6^{\circ}$ and $55.1^{\circ}$ , respectively. Namely, the surface of Ti-ATA-Cr is less hydrophobic than that of Ti-ATA. This excludes the possibility that hydrophobicity of Ti-ATA-Cr is responsible for its stability in water.
As high valent metal-based MOFs can be more stable under acidic solutions than neutral or basic environments. We have checked the pH values of the aqueous solutions containing different metals in the presence of Ti-ATA for Ti-ATA-Cr $\left(\mathrm{pH}=3.04\right)$ , Ti-ATA-Y $\langle\mathsf{p H}=4.12\rangle$ , Ti-ATA-La $\cdot\mathrm{pH}=3.68\dot{}$ , Ti-ATA-Ce $\mathrm{\Phi}^{\prime}\vert\mathsf{p H}=3.61\$ , Ti-ATA-Nd $\mathrm{\Phi}[\mathrm{pH}=3.54) $ and Ti-ATA-Tm $\langle\mathsf{p H}=3.64\rangle$ . We point out that the pH value remains stable $(\pm0.2)$ for a relative long time (4 days). When putting Ti-ATA in water with a pH value of ${\sim}3.92$ , it is unstable as we stated above. Therefore, we can conclude that pH value is not responsible for the stability of Ti-ATA-M in water.
We now examine the probable Cr sites in Ti-ATA-Cr and its implication on the stability of Ti-ATA-Cr in water. The comparison of the powder XRD patterns of as-prepared Ti-ATA and Ti-ATA-Cr indicated that the Cr atoms of Ti-ATA-Cr do not occupy the Ti sites of Ti-ATA [23–26]. To further confirm this point, we carry out a solvothermal reaction of a DMF $/C\mathrm{H}_{3}{\mathrm{OH}}$ solution containing Cr $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ , ATA and $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{i}{-}0\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ (i-OPr $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ isopropyl) with the $\mathrm{Cr/Ti}$ atom ratio of 0.16, to find that this reaction does not lead to a crystalline product (Fig. S7). Therefore, we think that Cr atoms of Ti-ATA-Cr do not replace Ti atoms of Ti-ATA but stay in the pores on the surface of Ti-ATA-Cr, which is most likely to keep the OATiAO bond from $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ attack. This is confirmed by FT-IR spectra (Fig. 4). The $_{0-\mathrm{Ti}-0}$ vibrations of Ti-ATA-Cr at $400{-}800\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ remain the same as those of as-prepared Ti-ATA, while the OATiAO vibrations of Ti-ATA disappeared after stirring in water for 4 days.
The XPS spectra were further carried out (Fig. S8). There are two obvious peaks at 577 and $587\mathrm{eV}$ for the $\mathtt{C r2p}$ spectrum, which are assigned to the $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}2\mathsf{p}3/2$ and 2p1/2, respectively [27,28]. In terms of N 1s, no obvious difference is observed except the small intensity decrease of Ti-ATA-Cr. With respect to O 1s, the peak $(533.2\mathrm{eV})$ assigned to chemisorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules increases for Ti-ATA-Cr compared to Ti-ATA. It is reasonable considering the fact that Ti-ATA-Cr has been stirred in water for 4 days. All the other peaks of the O1s spectra are at the same position with only a small difference in intensity. The peak at $530.2\mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to Ti-O, the one at $531.6\mathrm{eV}$ to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , and the one at $534.4\mathrm{eV}$ to adsorbed oxygen. It seems that either O or N in ATA does not interaction with $\mathsf{C r}^{3+}$ . As Ti-ATA-Cr is stable at $\mathsf{p H}=3$ , this fact also precludes the possibility that ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ exists as $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . Therefore, at present, we can just assume that ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ doesnot change the structure but stay atomically on the surface of Ti-ATA.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, the titanium-based MOF Ti-ATA is not so stable in water due to the attack of $\mathrm{{OH^{-}}}$ . However, its stability is greatly enhanced without sacrificing its porosity and photocatalytic activity by treating it with aqueous solutions containing metal ions $\mathbf{M}^{n+}$ with empty d orbitals (i.e., $\Upsilon^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ ) or partiallyfilled d orbitals (i.e., $\mathsf{C r}^{3+^{.}}$ ). The $\mathbf{M}^{n+}$ ions do not replace $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ as metal dots, but stay in the pores on the surface of Ti-ATA, which is most likely to keep the OATiAO bond from $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ attack.
